+ta cleanup --only drafts,staging     # Limit to: drafts, staging, goals, locks, archive, branches
+#### Pruning finished branches and worktrees
+
+Finished branches and worktrees are pruned for you; nobody has to remember. The `branches` group asks the VCS about each local branch's pull request (not `git branch -d`, which is fooled by squash merges) and deletes a branch without asking only when both of these hold:
+
+- its pull request is merged and the branch has no commits beyond the PR head, or its tip is already contained in the default branch; and
+- it is not the current branch or the default branch, and any worktree it is checked out in has no uncommitted or untracked files (that worktree is removed along with the branch).
+
+Everything else is a question, never an automatic deletion: a branch with an open PR, no PR, a closed-unmerged PR, commits beyond the PR head, or a worktree with unsaved files (the question lists the files).
+
+```bash
+ta cleanup --only branches --dry-run   # Show every branch, the evidence and the reason. Changes nothing.
+ta cleanup --only branches             # Prune the safe ones, ask about the rest
+```
+
+Every deletion prints the branch name and its tip commit, and is appended to `.ta/cleanup/branches-deleted.jsonl` and `.ta/audit.jsonl`. To get a branch back:
+
+```bash
+git branch feature/my-branch 3f2a9c1d...   # the tip from the log
+```
+
+The group runs by itself in two places: right after `ta draft watch` sees your pull request merge, and in the daemon's periodic cleanup (even when `auto = "off"`). It only runs the safe deletions there; questions wait for `ta cleanup`. Remote branches are never deleted (let your host do that, for example GitHub's "Automatically delete head branches"). A remote branch whose PR was closed unmerged is listed as a finding only, with the command to delete it yourself if you want to.
+
+prune_branches = true # false: stop pruning finished branches and worktrees automatically
